shoes  hello my name is Elis strablov and I'm a  professor at The Graduate School of  Business Stanford University  specializing in venture capital and  private equity  one of the classes I co-teach is Angel  and Venture Capital financing for  entrepreneurs and investors today are  five takeaways from this class  [Music]  success in startup investing is not just  due to luck as many believe but also  skills sweat perseverance this is my  spend over 100 hours on due diligence of  just one startup making a dozen  reference calls to check on the idea  Founders product Market  they debate startups opportunities and  risks with their partners  the skills of how to conduct due  diligence are critical as is the  appreciation that it is hot and often  unglamorous work  as an investors conduct such extensive  due diligence Founders need to get fully  prepared for it  as I summarize it to my students to be  Founders ask yourself all the questions  if you were an investor  then your aim is to have a great answers  to all these questions depth is  absolutely critical also identify your  weak spots investors avoid shallow  Founders who don't know the ins and outs  of their startup as my former student  who now is successful investor told me  if I come away from meeting the founder  without learning anything new it's a bad  sign  Founders should remember success is all  about execution most investors would  rather invest in a plus teams pursuing B  plus ideas rather than b plus teams  pursuing a plus ideas  [Music]  the third takeaway is that you need to  know the ins and outs of contracts in  the research space  these contracts determine how any  proceeds from startup's outcome are  determined that is who is paid what when  how and how the decisions and startups  life are made  we actually spent most of our course  discussing all these contracts in great  detail  Financial Security and startups are very  unusual if you compare them to  traditional debt and Equity securities  they need to take into account that many  startups fail  that startups raise many rounds of  funding from many investors  and their investors need patience  the takeaway is to understand this  contract and contractual terms really  really well  [Music]  importance of corporate governance that  is how decisions are made in startups  for example lead early stage VCS  typically become board members get  shareholder voting rights often they get  special Vita rights to prevent the start  of taking certain actions without their  approval  these rights help protect investors how  often minority shareholders in the  startup and are afraid they could be  expropriated by others such as the  founders Founders may also be concerned  that VCS May kick them out once they  control the startups board  understanding corporate governance its  implications and negotiations around it  is really a critical skill in the world  of startups  foreign  result is that valuation serves many  purposes apart from just finding out  what the security or company fair value  is especially for early stage startups  varying startups is often about how to  divide the future pie  the world of startups even uses its own  jargon instead of valuation used  elsewhere the post-money evaluation of  the pre-money evaluation is used for  startups in class we discuss at length  these and other valuation terms remember  one thing that the post money evaluation  is not the same as the fare or the  market value in fact my research shows  that post money evaluation is typically  higher than the fair value a result with  far-reaching practical implications  so if you take all these five takeaways  to hot and utilize them either as a  founder or as an investor then you are  much more likely to succeed in the world  of Angel bench capital investment in the  world of startups good luck  foreign  [Music]  if anybody would have told me even even  20 years ago that I would ever teach  French capital I would love but now that 